 of the next chapter, but even in this first chapter we will see that we can already do many subtle and difficult things − and even find open questions − with only the so-called “affine plane”. Affine geometry is a weaker structure than Euclidean geometry. Simply put: we won’t be working with anything but points and lines; the mathematical definition is given in Sect. I.XYZ at the end of the chapter. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBook Reviews A. Bultheel (https://people.cs.kuleuven.be/~adhemar.bultheel/WWW/EMS/r074.php)\nciteturn822search10 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.9 years ago; Crawled: last week; title: Geometry Revealed. ... Year: 2010 ... It first appeared in 2009, 21 years after the Geometry books. ... As Berger suggests in the introduction, one can pick up the book and open it at random and see if there is something that interests you. ... Moreover, if one is prepared to really think about some of the problems or follow Berger's suggestions and work out some of the proofs, some level of geometric training, for example being familiar with his Geometry books, is necessary or at least advisable. ... Some are just hand-drawn with hand-written text, others are reprinted from other publications and some are computer generated without reference.\nAuthor(s): Marcel Berger\ntitle: Geometry Revealed. A Jacob's Ladder to Modern Higher Geometry\nPublisher: Springer Verlag\nYear: 2010\nISBN: 978-3-540-70996-1 (hbk)\nPages: 847 pp.\nPrice (tentative): 31,79 € (net)\nURL: www.springer.com/mathematics/geometry/book/978-3-540-70996-1\nShort description:\nThis can be seen as a sequel to the classical Geometry I,II books by the author that appeared a couple of decades ago. It describes additional problems, theorems and proofs and more recent evolution. The style is colloquial and proofs conceptual. For more details the reader is referred to the literature.\nMSC main category: 51\nMSC category: 51-00  |  | \nReview:\n\nMarcel Berger is a well known and influential French geometer. His Géométrie I and Géométrie II appeared in 1977. English translations appeared in 1987. In the nineteenth century the millennium-long supremacy of Euclidean geometry was replaced by an outbreak of non-Euclidean geometries but in the course of the twentieth century, geometry had somewhat disappeared from the mainstream of mathematics, but near the end of that century, geometry got married to several other aspects of mathematics and a new impetus was given to the field. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSmooth surfaces (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/302505568_Smooth_surfaces)\nciteturn822search11 [wordlim: 200] Published: 16.7 years ago; Crawled: 5 months ago;   * February 2010 ...   * In book: Geometry Revealed (pp.341-407) ... Marcel Berger ... To read the full-text of this research, you can request a copy directly from the author.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeometryRevealed\u0000 (https://virtualmmx.ddns.net/gbooks/GeometryRevealed.pdf)\nciteturn822search13 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 8 months ago; Berger, *Geometry Revealed*, DOI 10.1007/978-3-540-70997-8_2,© Springer-Verlag Berlin Heidelberg 2010\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Complete Bibliography of The Mathematical (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=682641c2fe5739cdc4aa9761cad3d9c5dc71645d&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn822search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; 2009. ... Geometry revealed, by MarcelBerger. ... 2010.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGeometry Revealed - Toc (https://beckassets.blob.core.windows.net/product/toc/718617/9783540709961_toc_001.pdf)\nciteturn822search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.5 years ago; Geometry Revealed ... Marcel Berger, Lester J. ... Auflage 2010.\n--------------------------